---
const items = [
  {
    q: "What is Chinese food?",
    a: "A shared name for many regional cuisines shaped by geography and climate — from numbing Sichuan to delicate Cantonese.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should a first-time visitor eat in China?",
    a: "Start with Peking duck, xiaolongbao, mapo tofu, roujiamo, kung pao chicken and wonton soup, then follow the city guide.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much does food cost in China?",
    a: "Street snacks 8-30 RMB, casual meals 40-90 RMB per person, a full Peking duck dinner 120-220 RMB or more.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is Chinese street food safe?",
    a: "Pick busy stalls with fast turnover and high-heat cooking, drink bottled water, and carry allergy phrases in Chinese.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can vegetarians eat well in China?",
    a: "Buddhist restaurants, tofu, vegetable stir-fries, rice noodles and many dim sum work well. Say 'wǒ shì sù shí zhě' and look for the 'sù' sign.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I eat gluten-free in China?",
    a: "Possible but tricky — soy sauce has wheat, and noodles, dumplings and buns are everywhere. Order steamed fish, rice, plain veg and rice noodles, and show a written gluten-free note.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is Chinese food halal? Where can I find halal food?",
    a: "Most Chinese food isn't halal, but Xi'an, Ningxia and Xinjiang have strong halal traditions. Look for the green 'qīng zhēn' (清真) sign; lamb, beef and wheat dishes dominate.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the most common allergens in Chinese food?",
    a: "Peanuts, soy, wheat, sesame, shellfish and eggs appear constantly, and soy is nearly unavoidable. Carry a printed allergy card in Chinese and check with staff.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I order food if I do not speak Chinese?",
    a: "Use the phrase cards, point to a photo on this page, or show the Chinese text to staff.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I read a Chinese menu if I cannot read characters?",
    a: "Find picture menus, use a translation app camera, or show a dish name from this page. The cheat sheet above also helps.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is a typical Chinese meal structure?",
    a: "Meals are shared. Cold dishes first, then hot dishes in sequence, then rice or noodles. Order one dish per person plus one to share.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is dim sum and how do I order it?",
    a: "A Cantonese brunch of small steamed, fried and baked plates from carts or a tick-box menu. Order tea first, then baskets of three to four: har gow, siu mai, char siu bao, egg tarts. Go before noon.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is hot pot and is it beginner-friendly?",
    a: "A shared pot of boiling broth where you cook raw ingredients. Start with a half-spicy, half-mild 'yuānyāng' pot and dip in sesame oil with garlic.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the eight great Chinese cuisines?",
    a: "Sichuan, Cantonese, Shandong, Jiangsu, Hunan, Zhejiang, Fujian and Anhui — each tied to a region and its signature flavors.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is Chinese food always spicy?",
    a: "No. Sichuan and Hunan are hot, but Cantonese, Jiangsu and Shandong are mostly mild or savory.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between northern and southern Chinese food?",
    a: "The north is wheat-based (noodles, dumplings, breads) and savory; the south is rice-based, lighter, fresher and more seafood-focused.",
  },
  {
    q: "What do Chinese people eat for breakfast?",
    a: "Jianbing, baozi, congee, youtiao, soy milk and noodles, often from street stalls for 5-15 RMB.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I pay for food in China?",
    a: "Almost everywhere takes WeChat Pay and Alipay by QR, both now accepting foreign cards. Cash works but is rare — carry some for small stalls. Set up a payment app before you arrive.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I ask for the bill and split it?",
    a: "Say 'mǎi dān' (the bill) or make a writing gesture. Splitting is increasingly accepted — ask 'kěyǐ fēnkāi suàn ma?'. Tipping isn't expected.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I ask for no MSG or less salt?",
    a: "Say 'bú yào wèijīng' (no MSG) and 'shǎo fàng yán' (less salt). Most kitchens will accommodate.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is tap water safe to drink in China?",
    a: "No — drink bottled or boiled water, and use it for brushing teeth if sensitive. Restaurants serve tea or bottled drinks.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the best way to find good restaurants in China?",
    a: "Use Dianping, seek busy local spots with queues, and avoid tourist-trap places near attractions. Fast-turnover stalls usually mean fresh, safe food.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the basic chopstick rules?",
    a: "Don't stand them upright in rice, point with them, or spear food. Rest them on the holder or across your bowl.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the drinking etiquette at Chinese meals?",
    a: "'Gānbēi' means bottoms up, not just cheers. Lower your glass below others' when toasted. Decline politely with 'wǒ bù néng hē jiǔ'.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should a beginner avoid ordering?",
    a: "Skip offal, very spicy málà hot pot, and stinky tofu or mandarin fish until you're adventurous. Start with Peking duck, xiaolongbao, char siu, fried rice.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which food cities are worth a trip on their own?",
    a: "Beijing, Chengdu, Xi'an and Shanghai each deserve a stop; Guangzhou and Chongqing reward focused food days.",
  },
  {
    q: "How many days do I need in each food city?",
    a: "Plan 2-4 days: 2-3 for Shanghai or Guangzhou, 3-4 for Beijing, 3-5 for Chengdu.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need to tip in China?",
    a: "No. Tipping isn't expected in restaurants or taxis, and some places may refuse it.",
  },
];
---

<section id="faq" class="border-t border-line bg-white">
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-7xl gap-12 px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] lg:px-8 lg:py-24">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
        Quick answers before you travel.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-md text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
        The common questions travelers ask before planning a food trip through China —
        from paying and tipping to allergies, halal and chopsticks.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-line">
      {items.map((item, index) => (
        <details class="group border-b border-line py-5" open={index === 0}>
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-lg font-medium">
            {item.q}
            <span class="text-2xl font-light text-accent-600 group-open:hidden">+</span>
            <span class="hidden text-2xl font-light text-accent-600 group-open:inline">-</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-muted">
            {item.a}
          </p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": items.map((item) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": item.q,
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": item.a,
      },
    })),
  })} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 pb-20 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <blockquote class="max-w-3xl border-l-4 border-accent-600 pl-5 text-muted">
      <p class="leading-relaxed">
        "Chinese cuisine is an important part of Chinese culture and includes
        many regional traditions, the most influential being the Eight Great
        Cuisines of China."
      </p>
      <cite class="mt-3 block text-sm not-italic text-accent-600">
        — Wikipedia, "Chinese cuisine".
        <a
          href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_cuisine"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="underline hover:text-accent-700"
          >en.wikipedia.org</a
        >
      </cite>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</section>
